From: mscharged-port
Subject: New runtime: staging copies in whole 256 KiB blocks
Upstream-base: d664382f57002fbff143911c81b06b7e7a4b3ab4
Classification: host performance (feat/performance). Apply after 0124.

For every buffer copy Mesa's Intel Vulkan driver (blorp_buffer_copy ->
isl_surf_from_mem) searches a 2D surface shape for the copy's 16-byte block
count by trial division. A count with no small divisors costs thousands of
divisions: after 0123 the four staging copies of a frame still took about
0.75 ms of the render thread on a UHD 620. Copying whole 256 KiB blocks
(multiples of 16384 blocks) keeps the search to a few steps. The extra bytes
come from the same staging region and land past every range the frame reads.

diff --git a/lib/gfx/encoding.cpp b/lib/gfx/encoding.cpp
index eb799bb..bc786db 100644
--- a/lib/gfx/encoding.cpp
+++ b/lib/gfx/encoding.cpp
@@ -441,9 +441,12 @@ bool encode_frame_buffer_copies(wgpu::CommandEncoder& cmd, const FramePacket& fr
     if (size == 0) {
       return;
     }
-    // Whole 16-byte blocks keep the copy on the driver's widest path. The
-    // padding stays inside the region, past every range a command reads.
-    const uint64_t end = std::min<uint64_t>(AURORA_ALIGN(static_cast<uint64_t>(size), 16), capacity);
+    // Whole 256 KiB blocks: Mesa's Intel driver searches a 2D shape for every
+    // buffer copy by trial division of its 16-byte block count (thousands of
+    // divisions for an odd count); a multiple of 16384 blocks takes a few.
+    // The padding stays inside the region, past every range a command reads.
+    constexpr uint64_t CopyBlock = 256 * 1024;
+    const uint64_t end = std::min<uint64_t>(AURORA_ALIGN(static_cast<uint64_t>(size), CopyBlock), capacity);
     cmd.CopyBufferToBuffer(staging_buffer(frame.stagingBuffer), stagingOffset, dst, 0, end);
     copied = true;
   };
